# infrastructure/docker/env/observability.env.example -> copy to observability.env
#
# CONTAINER ENVIRONMENT for otel-collector. Not the interpolation file — that is ../.env.
# The Collector config itself is ../otel/collector.yaml; only the values that change per
# deployment live here, so the pipeline shape stays in git and reviewable.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the Collector forwards. All three are on the `observability` network and all three are
# behind the `observability` PROFILE — the Collector itself is not (ADR-024), so on a deployment
# that never enables the profile these exporters simply fail and the pipeline drops. That is the
# intended shape: telemetry fails soft, and NOTHING depends_on the Collector.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
KB_TEMPO_ENDPOINT=http://tempo:4318
KB_LOKI_ENDPOINT=http://loki:3100/otlp

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The single metric path.
# Prometheus SCRAPES the Collector's `prometheus` EXPORTER on this port. It is not
# prometheusremotewrite and it is not Prometheus's OTLP receiver:
#   * `--web.enable-otlp-receiver` is off by default, so an OTLP push silently goes nowhere;
#   * the CI metric-catalog diff must scrape ONE endpoint that has seen every process's data.
#     Under PHP-FPM and Celery prefork each request lands in a different process, so a per-service
#     /metrics returns one worker's fragment and the gate passes while checking almost nothing.
# Keep the default OTLP->Prometheus translation (UnderscoreEscapingWithSuffixes): it is what turns
# `kb.chat.requests` into `kb_chat_requests_total`, which is the name every rule and dashboard uses.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
KB_PROM_EXPORTER_ADDR=0.0.0.0:8889

# `basic` in production. `detailed` prints every span to stdout, which includes attributes — use it
# only in dev, and never on a host that ships stdout to a shared log store.
KB_OTEL_DEBUG_VERBOSITY=basic

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EVERY VARIABLE IN THIS FILE IS READ BY ../otel/collector.yaml — and that is checkable, in one
# command, from the repository root:
#
#   comm -23 \
#     <(grep -oE '^KB_[A-Z0-9_]+' infrastructure/docker/env/observability.env | sort -u) \
#     <(grep -oE 'env:KB_[A-Z0-9_]+' infrastructure/docker/otel/collector.yaml | cut -d: -f2 | sort -u)
#
# Anything it prints is a line that READS as configuration and configures nothing. It printed FIVE
# on 2026-08-11 and prints none now. All five were DELETED rather than wired, each for a reason
# recorded beside the setting it claimed to control:
#
#   KB_OTEL_MEMORY_LIMIT_MIB / KB_OTEL_MEMORY_SPIKE_MIB — collector.yaml now uses
#     limit_percentage/spike_limit_percentage, which read the container's cgroup limit at startup
#     and log the resolved MiB on every boot. The "MUST agree with the container limit in
#     compose.yaml (512M)" paragraph that used to sit here was false twice over: nothing compared
#     the two files, and editing this value to satisfy the agreement changed nothing whatsoever.
#     The agreement is now computed instead of asserted.
#   KB_TAIL_DECISION_WAIT / KB_TAIL_NUM_TRACES — `${env:X}` on an UNSET variable does not fail; the
#     field silently takes the component default. The sizing rule is arithmetic over three values
#     (num_traces, decision_wait, the policy list) that all live in collector.yaml, so splitting it
#     across two files makes it uncheckable from either. See the tail_sampling block there.
#   KB_OTEL_REPLICAS — unconsumable. Neither compose.yaml nor compose.prod.yaml gives
#     otel-collector a `deploy.replicas`, and the second replica it purported to configure is
#     unsafe until the load_balancing exporter exists. See collector.yaml's SCALING NOTE.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
